  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThe approach combines convex optimization with a greedy local refinement strategy. The problem's non-convexity arises from the multiplicative terms in the cross-correlation. To address this, we minimize the maximum overlap incrementally by focusing on individual overlapping shifts, formulate their contributions as convex constraints, and iteratively improve the solution.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n- Variables: h \u2208 [0,1]^n, \u2211h = n/2\n- Objective: min M s.t. \u2211h[i](1 - h[i + k]) \u2264 M, \u2200k\n- Substitute M with the maximum overlap and tighten constraints stepwise\n- Solve using CVXPY with quadratic overestimates\n- Feasibility projection: maintain sum constraint while keeping h \u2208 [0,1]\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\na. Initialize with initial_h_values if available\nb. Validate feasibility and compute initial overlaps\nc. For each iteration:\n   i. Compute current cross-correlations\n   ii. Identify shift k_max with highest overlap\n   iii. Formulate QP to minimize overlap at k_max with feasible h\n   iv. Solve QP and update h\nd. Continue until time budget exhausted or no improvement\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n- n_points = 70, overlap shifts = 139\n- Solve sequence of QPs with per-shift constraints\n- Use CVXPY with ECOS solver for QP\n- Iterate 500 steps with 2s/QP, total 1000s budget\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n- Expect ~500 iterations, each 2s: Total 1000s\n- Maintain feasibility via projection\n- Track best solution; return if stuck\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n- Check h \u2208 [0,1], \u2211h = 35\n- Compute overlaps, verify M reduction\n- Return best h, M, and n_points",
